WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.590
Speaker 1: Let's start today with a discussion
about the economy.

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:05.580
Is the economy a disaster?

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.220
Well, many on the right say that it is.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.710
Now we're going to look at the most important
metrics for evaluating how the economy is

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:13.710
doing.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:18.140
The reason I'm doing this is whether you're
on the left or on the right.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.180
My suggestion is don't make this political.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.920
Many on the right who say the Biden economy
is a disaster are making this political.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.250
And you know that while I have my political
opinions, I don't think we should have a death

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.250
penalty.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:38.440
I think abortion should be legal and accessible
and affordable in most cases.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.140
I believe in the decriminalization, if not
legalization of many drugs.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:48.460
These are opinions of mine when it comes to
things like gas prices, debt and deficit,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:49.930
the economy.

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:58.649
My preference is to take an extremely sober
empirical approach, and we can always predict

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:00.290
a recession is coming.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:06.310
The type of economic system we have, at least
historically, has always eventually gotten

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.210
to a recession or a depression.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.490
Could we enter into one tomorrow?

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.490
Sure.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.970
Will we probably get into one at some point?

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.500
Yeah, probably.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.110
But when people come to you, maybe with tears
in their eyes and then they say the Biden

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:25.930
economy is a disaster, I would challenge you
to say that.

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:32.740
Which underlying metric tells us that let's
look at the most important ones.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:44.040
GDP growth, the last few quarters, 3.2% growth,
2.6% growth, 1.3% growth.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:50.079
Very unremarkable, not mind blowingly explosive
the way we saw during the immediate COVID

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:52.920
recovery and not problematic.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.920
Right.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:02:00.470
I mean, you zoom out and you see GDP growth
has been in this relatively tight range.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:02.070
Big decline for COVID.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:08.890
Big recovery in that quarter after the big
decline and then just fine GDP growth numbers.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:11.830
So we said, is GDP indicative of an economic
problem?

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:13.280
No, it is not.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.069
We then go to the unemployment rate.

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:18.530
One of the lowest unemployment rates we've
had.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:21.800
The chart on the screen goes back before 1950.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.420
Looks like it starts around 1948.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.700
And we see periods where unemployment has
been down in the 3% range, a period in the

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:35.860
early fifties, a period in the late sixties,
touching the the very high threes around the

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:42.300
year 2000, the dot com boom and then spiking
right when COVID hit.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:50.200
And we are now down to 3.6% unemployment,
one of the lowest unemployment rates in history.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:56.250
So we say, well, the picture painted by GDP
and unemployment certainly is not indicative

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.020
of an economic crisis as of today.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.700
We then look at the inflation rate.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:05.250
But David, sir, everything is so expensive
because of the Biden inflation.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.659
Well, if you actually look, that inflationary
period started well before Biden, it has been

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:15.640
historically higher than we saw during the
period starting in the mid-nineties.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:22.549
But inflation has now declined, declined declined
to just below right around 5%, exactly where

00:03:22.549 --> 00:03:25.440
I predicted we would be towards the end of
the summer.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.530
I said somewhere between four and 5%.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:29.140
It seems like we will be there.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.450
This is not hugely shocking.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.450
If you've been following the trend line and
you see that while we still have higher inflation

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:40.560
than we've had, you have to go back to, you
know, around 2010, we had a little bit of

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.440
a spike in inflation.

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:49.099
And then you look at around 1990 and then
certainly in the mid seventies, very high

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:54.280
inflation, World War two era OC inflation
has been higher.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:55.450
It's coming down.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:59.379
So now we build the picture GDP, unemployment,
inflation.

00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:01.349
It doesn't look like a crisis.

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:03.549
We then look at the consumer confidence index.

00:04:03.549 --> 00:04:10.120
Now this is an opinion based index admittedly,
and you do see that when we were in the thick

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.689
of COVID, consumer confidence declined significantly.

00:04:13.689 --> 00:04:15.720
It is important.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:20.989
It is important to look at the Y axis here
and see that it goes from 96 to 102.

00:04:20.989 --> 00:04:22.729
This is a relatively tight range.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:28.300
It is absolutely true that right before the
dot com bust, consumer confidence was very

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.750
high.

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:34.520
And then in the midst of the 2829 recession,
consumer confidence was low.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:41.180
It got very low during COVID and it has now
recovered to a level that we saw around 2012

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:47.169
and to a level we saw during the recovery
from 2008 and in the early nineties.

00:04:47.169 --> 00:04:50.030
So consumer confidence has been low, but it
is climbing.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.330
Well, what about stock market performance,
David?

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.400
Let's look at that.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.030
S&amp;P500 4%, one year return.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:07.270
Not amazing, but certainly not a crisis year
to date, 11% return from the S&amp;P 500.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:12.070
The three year is now a 10% annualized return.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:13.960
Very, very healthy.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.610
You look at five year, 9% return and ten year
10% return.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:21.810
The stock market is basically fine.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:27.000
Yes, we see these erratic, erratic up and
down days sporadically.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.750
That's something that's been part of the stock
market.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:30.860
So we're not finding any red flags.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:35.850
Well, maybe unemployment is low and GDP is
okay and consumer confidence is increasing

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.820
and the stock market is doing fine.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:39.680
But what about wages?

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:47.660
Well, we look at the federal wage growth tracker
and you see that wage growth sustained now

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:55.230
starting about a year and a quarter ago has
been at some of the highest levels that we've

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:59.780
seen dating all the way back to 1980 wage
growth.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.210
But these are crappy jobs.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:06.960
Well, wages are going up and you actually
see very nice wage growth.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:10.990
So the fine we have many jobs, but they are
crap.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.050
Wage growth seems to be doing okay.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:18.720
We then look at the balance of trade and you
see that we still have a trade deficit.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:25.930
But after bottoming out a couple of years
ago, the trade deficit has been reduced, reduced,

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:26.930
reduced.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:30.270
It's really important to understand, if you
look at this chart, you'll see that we've

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:33.820
had trade deficits dating all the way back
to the mid seventies.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:40.810
We have had trade deficits in the United States
since 1976 because we have become a country

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:44.460
that imports a ton of oil and consumer products.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:45.620
Okay?

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:52.830
The trade balance is not particularly shocking
if you understand the American economy.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:55.449
And then lastly, well, what about interest
rates?

00:06:55.449 --> 00:06:58.910
Yes, we are seeing an increase in interest
rates.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.400
This is making mortgages more expensive.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:07.341
This is also making it so that you can get
5% in a high yield savings account, which

00:07:07.341 --> 00:07:08.699
is pretty damn attractive.

00:07:08.699 --> 00:07:11.650
I will tell you what you have to understand
about interest rates.

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:14.661
And we have a chart up on the screen where
you see extremely high in.

00:07:14.661 --> 00:07:16.620
Interest rates in the early eighties.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:21.979
And then you see extremely low interest rates,
almost zero from about 2008 up until about

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:23.660
2016.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:29.120
Interest rates in the US reached their highest
level in 1981 and you can see it on the chart

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:35.280
because the Fed raised rates to combat soaring
inflation and to try to stabilize the economy.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.169
That's the story of high interest rates in
81.

00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:44.740
Since then, we've seen interest rates basically
decline for 40 years, all the way down to

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:49.680
essentially zero levels in 2010 after the
global financial crisis.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.139
Again, in 2020, they dropped back to zero
after the COVID pandemic.

00:07:54.139 --> 00:07:59.449
And now interest rates are going back up to
a level that we saw in 2006.

00:07:59.449 --> 00:08:05.509
Still below where we saw right before 911,
well below what we had in the late eighties.

00:08:05.509 --> 00:08:10.530
So there is really no big dramatic story on
interest rates as well.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:16.800
So I continue to recommend when these economy
doom and gloom ers come to you and they start

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.650
telling you these stories about the Biden
economy, the first thing to say is, listen,

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:28.750
the less regulated the version of capitalism
we have, the more endemic to the system booms

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.080
and busts will be.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:37.120
There may well be in a recession or a depression
coming, but what current indicator is suggestive

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:38.120
of that?

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:42.349
Now, the one I didn't mention, which they
might go to is labor participation rate.

00:08:42.349 --> 00:08:44.920
The population of the country continues to
go up.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:51.820
And so it is true that at most times there
are more people than ever working and more

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.690
people than ever not working.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:58.360
It is not a particularly particularly reliable
indicator of economic health, and that's why

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.970
I didn't even include it.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:01.350
Ask them for the facts.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:02.100
Show them the receipts

